  "dead_pixel_fix_title": "Dead Pixel Repair Methods & Understanding",
  "dead_pixel_fix_intro": "When pixels get stuck, several approaches exist, some with better success rates than others. This guide explains what happens with stuck pixels and various methods people have tried." ,
  "dead_pixel_fix_method1_title": "Physical Pressure (Limited Success)",
  "dead_pixel_fix_method1_desc": "Some users report attempting to gently apply pressure around the affected area with a soft cloth. The theory is that mechanical pressure might help reset the stuck transistor. However, this method is risky and rarely successful. Always use extreme caution to avoid additional damage." ,
  "dead_pixel_fix_method2_title": "Power Cycle and Wait",
  "dead_pixel_fix_method2_desc": "Switch the display off, unplug it for a few hours and check again. Some stuck pixels clear on their own in the first days after they appear, so recheck before trying anything else. Never apply heat, moisture or force to a panel - it can cause permanent damage and may void your warranty." ,
  "dead_pixel_fix_method3_title": "Color Stimulation (Most Accessible)",
  "dead_pixel_fix_method3_desc": "The most commonly attempted method uses software to rapidly flash colors at the stuck pixel location. The theory is that sustained electrical stimulation might help reset the transistor's state. It doesn't always work, but this approach is completely safe and carries no risk of additional hardware damage. It's the method most worth trying first." ,
  "dead_pixel_fix_method4_title": "Warranty & Replacement (Most Reliable)",
  "dead_pixel_fix_method4_desc": "Most monitors include warranties covering manufacturing defects. Typically, manufacturers accept 1-3 dead pixels as normal, but if you exceed their threshold, professional replacement is often the most reliable solution. Contact your manufacturer's support to understand your specific warranty." ,
  "dead_pixel_fix_success_title": "Important Considerations",
  "dead_pixel_fix_success_note1": "Stuck pixels (showing color) have better repair prospects than dead pixels (completely black). Understand which type you have before attempting fixes.",
  "dead_pixel_fix_success_note2": "Results vary widely and aren't guaranteed. Age matters: pixels stuck for hours respond better than those stuck for weeks.",
  "dead_pixel_fix_success_note3": "Check your warranty first. Many manufacturers will replace displays if pixel issues exceed their acceptable defect thresholds.",
  "dead_pixel_fix_cta": "Start the Dead Pixel Test" ,
